WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.080
The digit four in forty-two is worth forty,

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.750
because of where it stands.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.630
Swap the digits and the same four is worth only four.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.380
A digit's position multiplies its value,

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.700
and writing that down turns digit puzzles

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:14.950
into simple algebra.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.030
Digit puzzles look like guessing games.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.300
But if you write a two-digit number as ten times its

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.150
first digit plus its second,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.950
most of the guessing disappears.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.850
A two-digit number is reversed, and the new number

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.200
is twenty-seven bigger.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.160
What can you say about its digits?

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.360
Pause and write both numbers using place value.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.520
Call the tens digit A and the units digit B.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:44.920
The number is ten A plus B.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.440
The reverse is ten B plus A.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.010
Subtract.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.660
The difference is nine B minus nine A, which is nine

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.290
times the gap between the digits.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:56.950
Twenty-seven is nine times three.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.550
So the units digit is three more than the tens digit.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.830
That gives fourteen, twenty-five, thirty-six,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.130
forty-seven, fifty-eight and sixty-nine.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.690
Check one: fifty-two minus twenty-five is twenty-seven.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:17.090
Adding a number to its reverse gives eleven A plus

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.940
eleven B: eleven times the digit sum.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.834
Forty-seven plus seventy-four is one hundred and

00:01:22.834 --> 00:01:26.140
twenty-one, which is eleven times eleven.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.320
Remember that a digit is not just any number.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.430
The first digit runs from one to nine, and the others

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:33.760
from zero to nine.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.960
Those limits often finish a puzzle.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.380
Write numbers using place value.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.400
Reversing and subtracting gives a multiple of nine.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.260
Reversing and adding gives a multiple of eleven.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:48.070
Notebook challenge: a two-digit number is four times the

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:48.920
sum of its digits.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:50.740
Find all such numbers.
